# Startup Session

Run at the **start of every working session** before writing or changing code. This rule **orchestrates** the ritual; sister rules carry the detail:

| Rule | Role |
|------|------|
| [readme-first-session.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/readme-first-session.mdc) | What docs to read (step 1) |
| [project-context.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/project-context.mdc) | Stack, layout, commands — if the project copied the template |
| [baseline-verification.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/baseline-verification.mdc) | Test/lint baseline before code edits (globs-triggered) |
| [engineering-workflow.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/engineering-workflow.mdc) | Dev loop after startup |
| [testing-tdd.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/testing-tdd.mdc) / [no-scaffold-code.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/no-scaffold-code.mdc) | Quality gates during execution |

## When to Run

**Full ritual** when:

- A new chat/session starts
- You switch branches or return after a long gap
- Task scope changes materially (frontend → backend, feature → infra)
- The user gives a multi-step or architectural request

**Abbreviated** (steps 1–2 only) for:

- Pure Q&A with no code changes
- Doc-only edits
- Single trivial follow-up in the same session (typo, one-line clarification)

When abbreviated, skip git baseline and baseline verification; still confirm scope.

## 1. Orient (read, don't guess)

Execute the checklist in [readme-first-session.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/readme-first-session.mdc).

Additionally:

- Read [project-context.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/project-context.mdc) if present and filled in
- Scan `.cursor/skills/` — load any skill matching the task domain
- Glance at open terminals before starting new dev servers
- Note which docs MCP / search you'll use for library questions (step 6)

## 2. Confirm scope (before touching code)

Restate in 1–3 sentences:

- **Goal** — what the user wants done
- **Surfaces** — apps/packages/dirs in scope
- **Out of scope** — what you will not change unless asked

If ambiguous or multiple valid approaches exist, ask **one** clarifying question before proceeding.

## 3. Establish git baseline

If the project is a git repo:

```bash
git status
git branch --show-current
git log --oneline -5
```

- Flag unrelated uncommitted changes before starting new work
- Note behind/ahead of remote when it matters
- Do not stack new work on unrelated dirty state without telling the user

If not a git repo, note that and proceed.

## 4. Baseline verification (code changes only)

When you will edit code, follow [baseline-verification.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/baseline-verification.mdc) — discover commands, run tests/lint **before** the first edit.

That rule activates via globs when code files are in context. For Q&A-only sessions, write `Baseline: skipped — no code changes` in the startup output.

## 5. Plan before editing

For non-trivial work:

- Outline the approach or use task tracking
- Identify affected files and trace data paths before changing them
- Search for existing patterns; reuse before new abstractions
- Multi-step work: implement directly or plan first — see [post-plan-deep-review.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/post-plan-deep-review.mdc)

## 6. Research before coding

- Consult up-to-date docs for libraries/frameworks/APIs — training data may be stale
- Retrieve secrets from the configured source; never hardcode
- Check `.env.example` when touching config or integrations

## Startup Output (required)

Emit before the first code edit (or before answering, for abbreviated Q&A):

```
## Session startup
- **Goal**: …
- **Scope**: …
- **Docs read**: …
- **Git**: branch / clean or dirty (or "not a git repo")
- **Baseline**: commands + pass/fail | skipped — no code changes | see baseline-verification
- **Plan**: one-line approach
- **Blockers**: none / list
```

Keep it short — evidence of orientation, not a report for its own sake.

## Rationalization Prevention

| Excuse | Reality |
|--------|---------|
| "The task is small, I'll skip startup" | Run steps 2 + 4 minimum for any code change. |
| "I remember this project" | Memory drifts. Read project-context and README. |
| "Tests were passing last session" | Re-run per baseline-verification. State changes. |
| "Pure question, but I'll tweak one line" | Scope changed — run baseline before that line. |
| "The user wants it fast" | Fast wrong is slower. Orient, then move with confidence. |
